# What line endings a committed file has, decided here rather than by whoever last saved it.
#
# WHAT THIS COST BEFORE IT EXISTED. A pull request arrived with three files rewritten in
# Windows line endings. Nothing in them had changed except the invisible byte at the end of
# every line, so a thirty-two line change was a 1,795 line diff, every reviewer read it as a
# rewrite, and it conflicted with everything else open against the same files. At least one
# researcher works from WSL, where an editor or a `git config core.autocrlf true` inherited
# from a Windows install does this without being asked, so it recurs until something in the
# repository decides the answer.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` is that decision, and the two halves do different jobs. `text=auto`
# lets git detect which files are text and normalise them to LF in the object database on
# the way in, so a CRLF working copy commits as LF whatever the contributor's
# `core.autocrlf` says. `eol=lf` fixes what is written back out on checkout, so nothing
# converts them to CRLF again on the next clone. Without the second, a Windows checkout gets
# CRLF working files and the next editor that rewrites one puts the diff back.
#
# WHY IT COVERS EVERYTHING RATHER THAN A LIST OF SUFFIXES. Every tracked file in this
# repository is text -- 292 Python, 133 JSON, 58 YAML, 8 Markdown, and the lock, the two
# TOML files and the two `py.typed` markers -- and the checked-in evidence and fixture JSON
# is exactly the kind of generated file a list of source extensions would have missed. A
# suffix list is also a thing somebody has to remember to extend, and the day it is not
# extended is the day the next 1,795 line diff arrives. git's own detection is what decides
# whether a file is text, so a binary added later is left alone without this needing an
# entry for it.
#
# NOTHING IN THE TREE MOVES WHEN THIS LANDS. Every tracked file was already LF, checked
# before this was written, so this changes no committed byte and fixes no existing damage.
# It is a guard against the next one, and the guard is the whole of what it is for.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, SAID SO THAT NOBODY READS MORE INTO IT. It says nothing about
# trailing whitespace, about a missing final newline, or about the encoding of a file, and
# it does not reformat anything already committed. It also does not reach a file a
# contributor has forced to be binary with a `-text` attribute of their own, which no file
# here has.
* text=auto eol=lf
